取不同步长：
$$\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
h & 10^{-2} & 10^{-4} & 10^{-6} & 10^{-8} & 10^{-10}\\\hline
\text{误差} & -9.0\times10^{-6} & -9.0\times10^{-10} & +2.8\times10^{-11} & -3.0\times10^{-9} & -5.8\times10^{-8}
\end{array}$$
$h$ 从 $10^{-2}$ 减到 $10^{-6}$，误差按 $h^2$ 缩小；再减小 $h$，截断误差虽然更小，但舍入误差 $\varepsilon/h$ 迅速增长，结果反而变差。最优步长 $h_*\approx(3\varepsilon/M)^{1/3}\approx1.1\times10^{-5}$，与表中现象一致——这就是数值微分的病态本质。

nodes.definitionEn:
A vector can be viewed as a list of numbers together with a fixed law of transformation: change the coordinates and the components change, while the arrow itself—an objective geometric object—stays the same. Promoting the idea of a list with a transformation law to several indices yields **tensors**:

- **rank 0** is a scalar: one number, unchanged by any coordinate transformation (temperature, mass, energy);
- **rank 1** is a vector: three components, each multiplied by one transformation derivative;
- **rank 2** is a matrix-like object: nine components $T_{ij}$, each index carrying its own transformation derivative—the stress, strain and inertia tensors are rank-2 examples.

The precise description is **multilinearity**: a rank-$n$ tensor is a map that takes $n$ vectors (one per direction) and returns a number, linearly in each argument; in three-dimensional space it has exactly $3^n$ components. The stress tensor illustrates the idea: given a unit normal $\boldsymbol{n}$, the traction on that face is $\boldsymbol{t}=\boldsymbol{\sigma}\boldsymbol{n}$, linearly in $\boldsymbol{n}$—a rank-2 tensor mapping vectors linearly to vectors.

What defines a tensor is not how many numbers it contains but **how they transform**: an array that does not obey the transformation law is not a tensor. Conversely, tensors describe **coordinate-independent, objective** objects (stress, curvature, electromagnetic fields): changing coordinates only changes the description, not the object itself. That is why tensor equations take the same form and hold in every coordinate system, and why tensors are the native language of continuum mechanics and relativity. The following entries develop the summation convention, tensor algebra, the metric tensor and Christoffel symbols, with the stress–strain tensor as an application.

Trigonometric functions are periodic and not monotonic, so they are not globally invertible. To obtain inverses we restrict each to a monotonic interval and a range, giving the **inverse trigonometric functions**:

- **arcsine** $y = \arcsin x$: domain $[-1, 1]$, range $\left[-\dfrac{\pi}{2}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]$;
- **arccosine** $y = \arccos x$: domain $[-1, 1]$, range $[0, \pi]$;
- **arctangent** $y = \arctan x$: domain $\mathbb{R}$, range $\left(-\dfrac{\pi}{2}, \dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)$.

Each is the inverse of the corresponding trigonometric function on its restricted interval, and its graph is the reflection of that restricted graph across the line $y = x$. They are indispensable in engineering and physics wherever an angle must be recovered from a ratio — phases, inclinations and bearings.

The **exterior derivative** $d$ is the differentiation operator on forms, $d: \Omega^k(M) \to \Omega^{k+1}(M)$. It is characterized by four properties, and **an operator satisfying them exists and is unique**:
(1) **Linearity**: $d(\omega + \eta) = d\omega + d\eta$ and $d(c\,\omega) = c\,d\omega$ for constants $c$;
(2) the **graded Leibniz rule**: $d(\omega \wedge \eta) = d\omega \wedge \eta + (-1)^{k}\,\omega \wedge d\eta$, where $k = |\omega|$ is the degree of $\omega$—the sign is determined by the **left factor** and must never be dropped or misplaced;
(3) **$d^2 = 0$**;
(4) **on functions it is the ordinary differential**: for $f \in C^\infty(M)$ and $v \in T_pM$, $df_p(v) = v(f)$, i.e. $df = \sum_i \frac{\partial f}{\partial x^i}\,dx^i$ in coordinates.
The special case $d(f\omega) = df \wedge \omega + f\,d\omega$ shows that $d$ behaves like a derivative with respect to products and is **not** $C^\infty(M)$-linear.

In local coordinates the formula can serve as a definition: for $\omega = \sum_I a_I\,dx^I$ (increasing multi-indices), $d\omega = \sum_I da_I \wedge dx^I = \sum_{I,j} \frac{\partial a_I}{\partial x^j}\,dx^j \wedge dx^I$: differentiate each coefficient and wedge with its $dx^I$. This also exposes the mechanism of $d^2 = 0$: $\frac{\partial^2 a_I}{\partial x^j \partial x^i}$ is symmetric in $j,i$ while $dx^j \wedge dx^i$ is antisymmetric, so the terms cancel pairwise—equality of mixed partials.

A form with $d\omega = 0$ is **closed**, and one of the shape $\omega = d\eta$ is **exact**. Since $d^2 = 0$, **exact forms are always closed**; the converse generally **fails**—the obstruction is purely topological and is measured by de Rham cohomology $H^k_{dR}(M) = \{\text{closed } k\text{-forms}\}/\{\text{exact } k\text{-forms}\}$, which counts the "holes" of the manifold. The classic example is $\omega = \frac{-y\,dx + x\,dy}{x^2+y^2}$ on the punctured plane $\mathbb{R}^2 \setminus \{0\}$: it is closed, but not the differential of any globally defined $\eta$, because its integral around the unit circle equals $2\pi \neq 0$, whereas an exact form integrates to zero around any closed curve. The **Poincaré lemma** gives the converse under a hypothesis: on any **star-shaped** (more generally, contractible) domain in $\mathbb{R}^n$, every closed form is exact. Note both the direction and the condition: contractibility is the precise "no holes" requirement, and the punctured plane is not contractible.

In $\mathbb{R}^3$ the exterior derivative subsumes classical vector calculus: the gradient corresponds to $df$ via $df(v) = \langle \nabla f, v \rangle$; a vector field $F$ corresponds to the 1-form $F^{\flat} = F_1 dx + F_2 dy + F_3 dz$, whose exterior derivative has exactly the components of $\mathrm{curl}\,F$, so that $\ast\,dF^{\flat} = (\mathrm{curl}\,F)^{\flat}$; and to the flux 2-form $\ast F^{\flat} = F_1\,dy\wedge dz + F_2\,dz\wedge dx + F_3\,dx\wedge dy$, whose derivative is $(\mathrm{div}\,F)\,dx\wedge dy\wedge dz$. Thus $\mathrm{curl}\circ\nabla = 0$ and $\mathrm{div}\circ\mathrm{curl} = 0$ are incarnations of $d^2 = 0$, and Green's, Stokes' and Gauss' theorems become the generalized Stokes theorem $\int_M d\omega = \int_{\partial M} \omega$. These dictionary entries use the metric and inner product (to identify vectors with 1-forms, to define $\ast$, and to pair $df$ with $\nabla f$); $d$ itself needs no metric—only the smooth structure.

Let $(M,g)$ be a **connected** Riemannian manifold and let $d$ be the Riemannian distance, i.e. the infimum of lengths of piecewise smooth curves joining the two points. The **Hopf–Rinow theorem** states that the following four conditions are **equivalent**:

① $(M,d)$ is **complete as a metric space** (every Cauchy sequence converges inside $M$);
② $M$ is **geodesically complete** (every maximal geodesic extends to a geodesic defined for all $t\in\mathbb R$);
③ there exists a point $p$ such that $\exp_p$ is defined on all of $T_pM$ (by the theorem this holds at one point iff it holds at every point);
④ every **closed bounded subset** of $M$ is compact (equivalently, every closed ball $\overline B(p,r)$ is compact).

If any one holds (hence all hold), then **any two points $p,q$ are joined by a minimizing geodesic** $\gamma$ with $L(\gamma)=d(p,q)$ — a genuinely shortest curve. Existence is not uniqueness: on $S^2$ antipodal points are joined by infinitely many minimizing geodesics.

The deepest point is ① $\iff$ ②: metric completeness (an analytic condition) is the same as extending geodesics forever (a geometric one). The implication ① $\Rightarrow$ ② is comparatively easy, while ② $\Rightarrow$ ① is the hard core, proved through the existence of minimizing geodesics. Note that ④ fails in general metric spaces: the closed unit ball of $\ell^2$ is complete and bounded but not compact; complete Riemannian manifolds are exactly the metric spaces in which closed bounded sets are compact.

Completeness cannot be dropped: on $M=\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}$ the points $(1/k,0)$ form a Cauchy sequence without limit, and between $p=(-1,0)$ and $q=(1,0)$ there is no minimizing geodesic, since $d(p,q)=2$ but every connecting curve has length $>2$ (semicircles around the origin give lengths tending to $2$).

